{
  "projection_type": "axonometric",
  "camera_view": { "rollDeg": 0, "heightFraction": 0.62 },
  "elements": [
    "Panel a) 1D: single horizontal black axis line with arrowheads at both ends, labeled x_1 at the right end",
    "Panel a): solid black dot at the origin of the x_1 axis",
    "Panel a): short vertical teal bar (thick tick) drawn through the origin dot, perpendicular to the axis — the 0-dimensional 'hyperplane' (a point) thickened for visibility",
    "Panel b) 2D: horizontal black x_1 axis with arrowheads both ends, label x_1 at right; vertical black x_2 axis with arrowheads both ends, label x_2 at top",
    "Panel b): solid black dot at the origin",
    "Panel b): thick teal vertical line segment lying along the x_2 axis through the origin — hyperplane of neuron 1",
    "Panel b): thick terracotta/orange horizontal line segment lying along the x_1 axis through the origin — hyperplane of neuron 2",
    "Panel c) 3D: black x_1 axis running to screen-right (arrowhead, label x_1), black x_2 axis running screen-up (double arrowheads top and bottom, label x_2), black x_3 axis running down-left toward viewer (arrowhead, label x_3)",
    "Panel c): dashed black continuations of the axes behind the planes (the leftward -x_1 dashed segment, the downward -x_2 dashed segment, and the dashed arrow heading up-right into the +depth quadrant)",
    "Panel c): light gray vertical plane (front-facing, spanning x_1 and x_2) — the x_1x_2 plane hyperplane",
    "Panel c): teal vertical plane spanning x_2 and x_3, perpendicular to x_1, intersecting the gray plane along the x_2 axis",
    "Panel c): terracotta/orange horizontal plane spanning x_1 and x_3, perpendicular to x_2, cutting through both vertical planes at the origin",
    "Panel c): origin dot where all three planes and axes meet",
    "Panel label text: a), b), c)",
    "Draggable input point x = (x_1, x_2, x_3) with drop lines/shadow to the axes (added for interaction)",
    "Shaded positive/negative half-space regions on each side of the active hyperplane"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "dimensionPanel",
      "type": "toggle",
      "teaches": "A hyperplane always has one dimension less than the input space: a point in 1D, a line in 2D, a plane in 3D.",
      "label": "Input dimension",
      "options": ["a) 1D", "b) 2D", "c) 3D"],
      "default": "b) 2D"
    },
    {
      "id": "orbit",
      "type": "orbit",
      "teaches": "The teal, orange and gray sheets in panel c are genuinely flat planes meeting at the origin, not a drawn cube — rotating shows each one edge-on.",
      "teaches_note": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "activeNeuron",
      "type": "toggle",
      "teaches": "Each neuron owns its own hyperplane; the network's regions come from several such boundaries at once.",
      "label": "Selected neuron",
      "options": ["Neuron 1 (teal)", "Neuron 2 (orange)"],
      "default": "Neuron 1 (teal)"
    },
    {
      "id": "w1",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "Changing the weight on x_1 tilts the selected hyperplane about the origin; the boundary is always perpendicular to the weight vector.",
      "label": "w₁ (weight on x₁)",
      "range": [-2, 2, 0.05],
      "default": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "w2",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "The weight on x_2 rotates the boundary in the x₁x₂ plane, so a single neuron can separate the space along any direction.",
      "label": "w₂ (weight on x₂)",
      "range": [-2, 2, 0.05],
      "default": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "w3",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "In 3D a third weight tips the plane out of the screen — the plane stays flat and still passes through the origin unless the bias moves it.",
      "label": "w₃ (weight on x₃)",
      "range": [-2, 2, 0.05],
      "default": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "bias",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "The bias translates the hyperplane off the origin without rotating it, shifting which inputs fall in the positive region.",
      "label": "bias β",
      "range": [-2, 2, 0.05],
      "default": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "dragPoint",
      "type": "drag",
      "teaches": "An input's class is decided by the sign of w·x + β for each neuron — moving the point across a boundary flips that neuron's decision and can flip the final output class.",
      "teaches_note": ""
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "a) One dimension: a point",
      "narration": "On a single number line the neuron's boundary w₁x₁ + β = 0 is just one point. Everything right of it is positive, everything left is negative.",
      "state": { "dimensionPanel": "a) 1D", "activeNeuron": "Neuron 1 (teal)", "w1": 1, "w2": 0, "w3": 0, "bias": 0 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Bias slides the point",
      "narration": "Increasing the bias moves the dividing point along x₁ without changing which side is positive — the shaded regions slide with it.",
      "state": { "dimensionPanel": "a) 1D", "bias": 0.9 }
    },
    {
      "title": "b) Two dimensions: a line",
      "narration": "With two inputs each neuron's boundary is a line through the origin. The teal neuron uses only x₁, so its line is vertical; the orange neuron uses only x₂, so its line is horizontal.",
      "state": { "dimensionPanel": "b) 2D", "activeNeuron": "Neuron 1 (teal)", "w1": 1, "w2": 0, "w3": 0, "bias": 0 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Tilting the teal line",
      "narration": "Adding weight on x₂ rotates the teal boundary. The two neurons now carve the plane into four regions instead of the original quadrants.",
      "state": { "dimensionPanel": "b) 2D", "activeNeuron": "Neuron 1 (teal)", "w1": 1, "w2": 0.8, "bias": 0 }
    },
    {
      "title": "c) Three dimensions: a plane",
      "narration": "In 3D the same equation describes a flat plane. Orbit to see the gray, teal and orange planes meeting at the origin, each the boundary of one neuron.",
      "state": { "dimensionPanel": "c) 3D", "activeNeuron": "Neuron 1 (teal)", "w1": 1, "w2": 0, "w3": 0, "bias": 0 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Tipping the plane and testing a point",
      "narration": "Weight on x₃ tips the teal plane out of the screen. Drag the input point through space and watch each neuron's sign — and the combined output class — switch as it crosses a plane.",
      "state": { "dimensionPanel": "c) 3D", "activeNeuron": "Neuron 1 (teal)", "w1": 1, "w2": 0.4, "w3": 0.9, "bias": 0.3 }
    }
  ],
  "geometry_notes": "World units: all three panels share one coordinate frame with axes spanning ±3 along each drawn axis; axis arrows tip at ±3.0 with cone heads ~0.18 long. Only one panel is visible at a time (dimensionPanel toggle), each centred at the origin.\n\nPanel a): single horizontal line along +X/−X from (−3,0,0) to (3,0,0), double arrowheads, label 'x₁' placed just below-right of the +X tip. Black origin dot radius ~0.09. The teal 'hyperplane' is a vertical bar centred on the boundary point, extending y ∈ [−0.45, 0.45], width ~0.09, colour teal #8CCFC9.\n\nPanel b): black x₁ axis along X from −3 to +3 (label 'x₁' below-right of +X tip), black x₂ axis along Y from −3 to +3 (label 'x₂' above-right of +Y tip), both with arrowheads at both ends. Teal hyperplane of neuron 1 is a thick bar (thickness ~0.12) lying ON the x₂ axis from y=−2.2 to +2.2 (i.e. the line w=(1,0) ⇒ x₁=0, vertical). Orange hyperplane of neuron 2 is a thick bar lying ON the x₁ axis from x=−2.2 to +2.2 (w=(0,1) ⇒ x₂=0, horizontal). Origin dot drawn on top in black. Half-space shading: light tint of the owning colour at ~12% opacity.\n\nPanel c) — read directly off the source: x₁ points to screen-RIGHT (+X, solid arrow at right, dashed thin line continuing to the left behind the planes, label 'x₁' at the far right just below the axis). x₂ points screen-UP (+Y, solid arrow up, second arrow head at the bottom, label 'x₂' near the top just left of the arrow; the lower portion drawn dashed where it passes behind the orange plane). x₃ points DOWN-LEFT toward the viewer (solid arrow, label 'x₃' at its lower-left tip); the opposite −x₃ direction (up-right, receding) is drawn as a DASHED arrow. Planes: (1) light-gray plane #C7CBCD at ~70% opacity is the x₁x₂ plane, standing vertical and facing the viewer, roughly 4.4 wide (X) × 4.0 tall (Y), slightly offset so it reads as a background card; (2) teal plane #8CCFC9 at ~75% opacity is the x₂x₃ plane, perpendicular to x₁, 4.0 tall (Y) × 3.6 deep (Z), cutting the gray plane along the x₂ axis and extending above and below it; (3) terracotta/orange plane #D2704E at ~85% opacity is the x₁x₃ plane, horizontal, 4.8 (X) × 3.6 (Z), passing through the origin and slicing both vertical planes. Black origin dot at (0,0,0) drawn in front of all planes.\n\nMath: each neuron's pre-activation is z = w₁x₁ + w₂x₂ + w₃x₃ + β; the hyperplane is z = 0 with unit normal w/|w|; positive half-space shaded in the neuron's colour at ~18% opacity, negative side left unshaded. The draggable input point is a black sphere (radius 0.12) constrained to |x| ≤ 2.6, with thin dashed drop-lines to each axis; a readout shows sign(z₁), sign(z₂) and the resulting output class.\n\nPalette: black #1A1A1A for axes/labels/dots, teal #8CCFC9, terracotta #D2704E, gray #C7CBCD, white background, panel letters 'a)' 'b)' 'c)' in black at the upper-left.",
  "notes": "Render with an orthographic camera in a standard isometric pose (works for all three panels; 1D/2D panels appear face-on because their geometry lies in the XY plane — snap the camera to a front-on view when dimensionPanel is a) or b), and ease back to the isometric pose for c). Axes as THREE.Line/CylinderGeometry shafts plus ConeGeometry arrowheads; dashed segments via LineDashedMaterial with computeLineDistances(). Labels (x₁, x₂, x₃, a/b/c) as CSS2DRenderer or sprite labels with MathJax-style italic subscripts so they stay screen-facing and crisp. Planes as PlaneGeometry with DoubleSide, transparent, depthWrite:false, and polygonOffset to avoid z-fighting where the three planes intersect; render order gray → teal → orange so the orange horizontal plane reads in front as in the source. Half-space shading: reuse the plane meshes offset slightly along the normal, or a shader colouring each side by sign(w·x+β). The active hyperplane (activeNeuron) gets a brighter edge outline; inactive ones dim to ~40% opacity. Drag the input point with a raycast onto a plane parallel to the view through the point's current position; clamp to the domain. Slider updates recompute the plane's quaternion from the normalized weight vector and translate it by −β/|w| along the normal; animate with a short lerp so the tilt is visibly continuous. Disable/grey the w₃ slider outside the 3D panel and w₂ outside 2D/3D."
}

